/* semver — is this a well-formed version, and does the book agree with the spec?

   Every other pinned example in this corpus records whatever the program
   happened to print. This one is different: semantic versioning is specified
   by somebody else, at semver.org, and the cases in
   `examples/assets/versions.txt` carry the answer the SPEC gives. The program
   compares its own verdict against that answer and leaves with 1 if the two
   ever disagree.

       cufet examples/parsing/semver.cufe

   That is the only defence this corpus has against being confidently,
   consistently wrong. Two backends agreeing proves they agree; an answer key
   nobody here wrote is what makes agreement mean correct.

   The exit contract:

       0   the validator agreed with the spec on every case
       1   it disagreed on at least one
       2   the case file could not be read

   ── Why this is a pattern program ──

   ★★ A version is a SHAPE, and the fiddly parts of the shape are exactly where
   a hand-written check goes wrong. "No leading zeroes" is the one everybody
   forgets: `01.1.1` is not a version, and neither is `1.2.3-0123`, because a
   numeric identifier may not be padded. Spelled as a pattern that rule is
   `0|[1-9]\d*` — either a bare zero, or a digit that is not zero followed by
   whatever. Spelled by hand it is three branches and an off-by-one.

   ⚠ **It validates and cannot extract.** A pattern gives back a fact, never
   the text it matched, so this says whether a version is well-formed and can
   say nothing about which part is the major. That is the book's shape rather
   than a gap in this program — and for the job the release checklist actually
   needs, "is this a version" is the whole question.

   Exercises anchors, `\d`, `\w`, a class ending in a dash, alternation,
   nested groups, `*` and `?`, and an escaped `\.` and `\+`.
*/

Pull a book on regex.
    /* The version core: three dot-separated numeric identifiers, none of them
       padded with a leading zero. */
    Define regex core as [^(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)$].

    /* The whole grammar, pre-release and build metadata included.

       ★ A pre-release identifier is a numeric one (unpadded) or one carrying at
       least one non-digit — that is what `\d*[a-zA-Z-][\w-]*` says, and it is
       why `1.0.0-0A.is.legal` is a version while `1.0.0-0123` is not.

       ⚠ Build metadata is laxer than pre-release ON PURPOSE: the spec allows
       leading zeroes there, because build metadata is not compared. Writing one
       pattern for both would have been tidier and wrong. */
    Define regex whole as
        [^(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)(-((0|[1-9]\d*|\d*[a-zA-Z-][\w-]*)(\.(0|[1-9]\d*|\d*[a-zA-Z-][\w-]*))*))?(\+([\w-]+(\.[\w-]+)*))?$].

    Bind void to usage:
        State "usage: semver".
        State "".
        State "checks every case in examples/assets/versions.txt against the spec's answer".
        State "leaves with 0 if they all agreed, 1 if any disagreed, 2 on a bad file".
    Done.

    Bind void to play:
        Define target as "examples/assets/versions.txt".

        If the number of the arguments is greater than 0:
            Cast usage.
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define lines as a series of text.
        Try to:
            The lines becomes read all lines from the file target.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            State "semver: {target}: {the message of the failure}".
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define checked as 0.
        Define disagreements as 0.

        For each line in lines, repeat:
            If the length of line is 0, skip.

            Define parts as line split by " ".
            Define expected as item 1 of parts.
            Define version as item 2 of parts.

            Define ours as cast whole on (version).
            Define theirs as expected is "valid".
            Increment checked by 1.

            /* The core alone is reported beside the full answer, because the
               difference between them IS the pre-release and build grammar —
               and seeing `1.0.0-alpha` fail the core while passing the whole is
               the clearest way to show what those brackets buy. */
            Define plain as cast core on (version).

            Define mark as "  ".
            If ours is not theirs:
                The mark becomes "!!".
                Increment disagreements by 1.
            Done.

            State "{mark} {version}: we say {ours converted to text}, the spec says {theirs converted to text} (core alone: {plain converted to text})".
        Done.

        State "".
        State "{checked converted to text} case(s) checked against the spec".
        State "{disagreements converted to text} disagreement(s)".

        If disagreements is greater than 0, exit with 1.
        Exit.
    Done.

    Cast play.
Done.
